WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.612
Require authenticated access at the cluster boundary and authorize clients for specific models or service classes.

00:00:07.612 --> 00:00:13.050
Protect APIs with encryption in transit, rate limits, and network controls.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:22.837
Segment user-facing gateways, management interfaces, storage, and worker traffic so compromise of one component does not expose the entire control plane.

00:00:22.837 --> 00:00:31.319
Store service credentials in a managed secrets system, rotate them, and avoid embedding them in images or scripts.

00:00:31.319 --> 00:00:38.497
Patch operating systems, GPU drivers, runtimes, front ends, and orchestration components through a controlled process.

00:00:38.497 --> 00:00:48.719
Remove one node from service, apply the change, test health and representative requests, and expand the rollout only when results are acceptable.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:58.071
Maintain rollback packages and configuration snapshots because a syntactically successful update can still change model behavior, memory use, or throughput.

00:00:58.071 --> 00:01:02.204
Monitor from the request down to the hardware.

00:01:02.204 --> 00:01:09.381
Track availability, error rates, queue time, time to first token, generation rate, and end-to-end latency.

00:01:09.381 --> 00:01:16.558
Correlate those signals with GPU utilization, VRAM, CPU, RAM, disk, network, power, temperature, and throttling.

00:01:16.558 --> 00:01:25.475
Health checks should detect whether a process is merely running and whether it can actually answer a model request.

00:01:25.475 --> 00:01:31.783
Alert on user impact and sustained resource conditions rather than every brief fluctuation.

00:01:31.783 --> 00:01:33.740
Prepare for incidents.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:39.178
Preserve useful logs without retaining sensitive prompts longer than policy allows.

00:01:39.178 --> 00:01:48.095
Define how to isolate a node, revoke credentials, stop abusive traffic, restore a model version, and communicate service degradation.

00:01:48.095 --> 00:01:55.272
Review cost and energy consumption alongside performance; idle accelerators and overloaded cooling are operational signals.

00:01:55.272 --> 00:02:07.669
A secure cluster is one whose normal state is visible, whose changes are controlled, and whose operators can recover from failure without improvising access or data handling.
